{"kind":"assert","text":"NF-5 ruling on df42ed84: one rule ends each case. A retired store\nstarts no transaction. No grace, no timeout, no new signal.\n\nThe rule. After `close` or `versionchange`:\n- An IndexedDB transaction that exists ends by its own result.\n- Nothing new starts on that store: no outcome row, no segment, no\n  read back, for any identity.\n- The database closes when the existing transactions end. No\n  network promise holds it.\n\nQuestion 1. No. An identity that is delivering gets no transaction\nafter the retirement. So `Custody` needs no signal that the deliver\nbegan, and the API gains nothing.\n\nQuestion 2. The database closes at once after the existing\ntransactions end. A network result that arrives later is not\nrecorded. The stored record stays as it was written, `sent`, with\nthe exact envelope. `Custody` gives its existing storage answer for\nthe result that it did not keep. It claims no acceptance and clears\nno slot.\n\nQuestion 3. A read after the retirement refuses, also when the\nwrite committed. `Custody` then answers that the envelope is not\nkept, and submits nothing for it. Check the bytes inside the write\ntransaction, as you propose, so that the write and its check are\none transaction.\n\nOne more line. `Custody` starts no POST after its store retired. A\nPOST that already left continues, because nothing recalls it.\n\nWhy this is safe. Each case leaves the store in a state that the\nlost-reply path already recovers: a record `sent` with no outcome,\nor a kept envelope that was never sent. The next load finds the\nrecord, and Check settles it. That is the path of the NF-7 witness.\nState in the note which existing recovery covers each case.\n\nThis fixes the words of 14609eb8: \"bookkeeping that was already\ndispatched\" means a transaction that exists.\n\nYour four witnesses are accepted. The third one shows the exact\n`sent` record and the storage answer, with no outcome row.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}